## Executive summary

The Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority is about to procure the replacement of Dulles Concourses C and D — trade press reports the announced IAD transformation at more than 5 million square feet and above $20 billion — on capital MWAA has not yet fully financed.[^1] The use-and-lease capital plan also contemplates, on a schedule MWAA has not published, an eventual replacement of the DCA Terminal 1 main concourse.[^11] The question in front of the Board is not whether to write a "quiet airport" policy. It is whether MWAA will bind an intelligibility standard into the Design Manual before the Concourse C/D consultant scope hardens — or bind it later, as change orders, on airline-recoverable rates that already forecast IAD signatory cost per enplanement rising from a $12.77 base in the 2025A forecast toward $20.71 by 2030.[^2]

The right instrument exists. It is called the MWAA Design Manual, which the Authority itself describes as "a mandatory guide with the force of law on the airport property."[^3] What the manual currently contains — whether it already carries binding STI, LEQ, RT60, or PA-zoning language — is not public. That diligence read is the first deliverable of the drafting authorization the Board should give.[^12]

The right numbers exist. NFPA 72 sets voice-alarm intelligibility of average STI ≥ 0.50 per Acoustically Distinguishable Space and ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations, verified in occupied conditions per IEC 60268-16 (STIPA); ACRP Research Report 175 recommends the same floor with a signal-to-noise ratio ≥ 10 dB(A) for pier-style departure lounges.[^4] The right federal accessibility floor exists: 49 CFR 27.71 makes visual paging a grant-eligibility condition, and 14 CFR 382.53 obliges air carriers to give passengers with vision or hearing impairment the same information available to others.[^5] DOT rulemaking on accessible information under Part 382 is moving toward *more* real-time information, not less.[^13]

What the ledger does not carry — and what this article refuses to sell — is peer-reviewed measurement that a "silent airport" policy reduces missed boardings, missed connections, or IROPS recovery time. It does not exist. London City, Helsinki, Amsterdam Schiphol, and Barcelona publish governance rules, not performance numbers.[^6] Even San Francisco's headline reduction is a mechanism story: the traceable figures are 47% fewer paging events and 60% less announcement duration, achieved because SFO physically disabled airline access to boarding-area paging and centralized non-routine announcements at its own desk.[^7]

The recommendation is therefore not a "quiet-by-design standard" — that flag lands sideways at post-January-29 DCA — but a **Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard**: mandatory intelligibility floor, mandatory redundant channels (audible, visual/digital, staffed) for every safety-critical and boarding-critical message, an explicit PA governance model with an IROPS carve-out, a tenant-systems chapter reaching airline podium audio and concession televisions, and a two-airport variance clause. Priced components size to $6M–$24M one-time; a conservative envelope including unpriced PA head-end reprogramming and commissioning is $40M — roughly $0.02–$0.09 per IAD enplanement, or 0.10 to 0.43 percent of IAD's projected 2030 signatory CPE.[^8]

