## Fort Lauderdale, January 6, 2017, ninety minutes after the first shot

Five dead at initial contact. Six wounded. Approximately ninety minutes later, false reports of a second shooter rippled outward through Terminal 2 and beyond, and passengers ran. The stampede put more than fifty additional people in the hospital. The National Policing Institute's after-action review, published in September 2017, records the numbers and describes a chaotic self-evacuation across all four terminals.[^9] Independent reporting on the response added a finding of radio and communications failures across the responding agencies.[^9a]

Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International Airport does not appear in any silent-airport case study. It should be the first one. The injuries in hour two were caused by the absence of an intelligible, authoritative voice reaching a crowd already in motion — that reading is the article's, not the AAR's, though the report's own record of chaotic self-evacuation is where the reading begins. Every airport that permits its acoustic environment to be assembled by accretion — one PA head-end, one airline podium at a time, one gate handheld carried onto speakerphone, one concession television replacing the last one — is running the same experiment.

## The peer canon has no numbers; the fire code has all of them

The peer set MWAA is being asked to emulate — London City, Helsinki, Amsterdam Schiphol, Copenhagen, Barcelona El Prat — is a set of governance rules. London City removed final-call and paging in 2008 and points passengers to information screens.[^10] Helsinki did the same in 2015. Schiphol tells deaf and blind passengers to notify their airline "at least 48 hours before departure,"[^10a] a pre-notification model that sits squarely against the effective-communication requirements of 49 CFR Part 27 on the U.S. side of the Atlantic. None of these operators publish measured STI, LEQ, or intelligibility numbers. Their policies tell an airport what to write down. They do not tell an airport what to measure.

The measurements live in the fire code. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets the intelligibility floor for Emergency Communication Systems — STI ≥ 0.50 average per ADS, ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations — and IEC 60268-16:2020 defines the STIPA field protocol a commissioning agent executes with a talkbox and a calibrated meter.[^4] NFPA 72 is the national voice-alarm intelligibility standard incorporated into the model codes Virginia adopts through the Uniform Statewide Building Code. The quiet-airport movement and the fire code have been converging on the same terminal from opposite directions for a decade. The public record does not establish whether MWAA's Design Manual already writes it down; the first act of the amendment authorization should be to find out.[^12]

## Alarm fatigue: the mechanism transfers, the numbers do not

Hospitals learned this earlier and paid worse tuition. Boston Medical went from roughly a million monitor alarms per week to about 400,000 by eliminating low-value categories. Johns Hopkins cut 63% of ICU alarms with one threshold change. The Joint Commission elevated alarm-fatigue reduction to a National Patient Safety Goal because 86 to 99.5 percent of physiologic monitor alarms are false, and clinicians trained by the environment to ignore them do so at the moment the environment produces a true one.[^14] The medical numbers do not transfer to a terminal. The mechanism does. A holdroom that pages constantly is running the alarm-fatigue experiment on its passengers, and the message that finally matters arrives at ears that stopped listening two hours ago.

## Atlanta, December 17, 2017: the redundancy floor is federal

On a Sunday afternoon, Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International lost commercial power for approximately eleven hours. The FAA control tower retained power; the terminal did not. Roughly 1,500 flights were grounded, and DOT later investigated about a hundred tarmac delays, including aircraft stuck for more than seven hours.[^13a] Any airport whose PA head-end, mass-notification server, and FIDS backbone run on the same terminal commercial feed inherits that failure envelope. The lesson is not aspirational. Emergency communication head-ends, the public-address backbone, and mass-notification servers must run on independent UPS and generator power — specified separately from terminal life-safety load — or the standard collapses on the first bad day.

The redundancy floor is not a design preference either. 49 CFR 27.71 requires airport operators receiving federal financial assistance to display PA messages on video monitors so passengers with hearing impairments do not miss information others receive.[^5] 14 CFR 382.53 puts the same obligation on air carriers, which pulls airline gate-podium audio and airline-club audio into scope and refutes any tenant carve-out.[^5] ADAAG 4.28.3 is explicit that audible and visual alarms are both required, and that a visual signal can only be observed within the space it occupies.[^15] "Reduce PA, add screens" does not satisfy the accessibility floor as written. "Govern PA, harden every channel" is the design that does.

## The DCA overlay, honestly stated

On January 29, 2025, PSA/American 5342 and a U.S. Army UH-60L collided on final approach to DCA. Sixty-seven people were killed. The NTSB's final report, issued February 2026, assigned probable cause to the FAA — specifically, FAA placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach, compounded by unheeded prior recommendations. The FAA restricted helicopter operations near DCA within days, and on March 14, 2025, removed the segment of Helicopter Route 4 between Hains Point and the Woodrow Wilson Bridge from published charts.[^16]

Terminal-side communications were not implicated in the NTSB probable cause. Say that on the page, before anything else about DCA. The reason this article is not framed as "quiet by design" is that framing would land, at an airport where the January 2025 memorial file is still open, as an aesthetic decision. Frame the same standard as *intelligibility by design*, present the emergency-communication and accessibility tier first, and its most publishable outcome becomes the ability to move a crowd with a voice message when it matters. That is the frame the Board can defend on a stage that now, per July 2026, includes the President and the U.S. Transportation Secretary.[^1]

## Concourse E, fall 2026: MWAA doesn't own the microphone

Concourse E opens in a few months. Fourteen new United gates, direct AeroTrain access, a 40,000-square-foot United Club — delivered inside a Tier 2 program of roughly $898 million with about $235.8M in federal grant support.[^17] Picture the first winter irregular-operations evening in that building. A ground stop hits. The loudest instrument in the holdroom is not the MWAA public-address head-end. It is the gate agent's handheld radio on speakerphone, layered over the club's entry audio, a TSA callout, and a set of concession televisions. One operating estimate in this run — a COO figure, not a metered number — puts the MWAA head-end at roughly 30–40% of the audible surface in a large concourse on a peak day; a separate brief on tenant sound, working from the other side of the equation, implies a head-end share closer to 60–70%. The two estimates disagree on the number. Four briefs agree on the qualitative claim: most of the audio in the room is not on MWAA's PA.[^18]

A standard that governs only MWAA-owned zones does not reach the loudest voices in the room. Which is why the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard must carry a **tenant-systems chapter**: fit-out acoustic criteria, re-verification after any envelope penetration, television and background-music governance, and a defined interface with the airline Use-and-Lease Agreement that took effect January 1, 2025.[^19] The SFO mechanism is transferable — audio-matrix modification plus a centralized announcement desk — but the SFO *headline* is not. Cite 47% fewer paging events and 60% less announcement duration; do not cite the self-reported 40% noise reduction or the 90 minutes per day.[^7] Dubai's 10-month, airline-by-airline negotiation, which capped announcements at two per flight across three terminals with no reported effect on on-time performance, is the ceiling of what governance alone can achieve.[^20]

The airline response is not theoretical. Denver's reported quiet-zone initiative was blocked by its dominant carrier — a single-source claim, but a directionally credible one.[^21] IAD's dominant carrier is United, which co-announced Concourse E and the July 2026 transformation. DCA's is American, at approximately 53% of DCA traffic at year-end 2024 in a slot-controlled airport where a missed departure means a lost slot, not a delayed passenger — one of ten beyond-perimeter exemptions the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 authorized and DOT awarded in December 2024.[^22] The standard that United negotiates into Concourse E's specification set becomes the baseline for C/D by force of precedent. The standard MWAA writes for DCA needs an explicit safety-and-IROPS carve-out, or American will use the first irregular-operations event to invalidate it in front of the Board.

## Timing, arithmetic, and the one instrument that already exists

The Design Manual is the instrument. Seven volumes, 2020 edition, updated annually, with the Authority's own framing: "a mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property."[^3] The right amendment is a Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard with a companion Specification and Estimating & Scheduling section. Management's best understanding of the adoption window — the current Board officer term, effective for all scopes not yet advertised — should be verified against the published C/D procurement schedule. Concourse C/D is inside that window on any reasonable read; DCA Terminal 1 is not on a public schedule and should be addressed at the point its programming is authorized.

The arithmetic is the least contested piece, once presented honestly. Acoustic ceiling packages price at roughly $5–$15 per square foot in new construction and $18–$45 per square foot as retrofit. Applied to Concourse E's roughly 435,000 square feet at the realistic 50% scope (holdrooms and circulation, not back-of-house), avoided rework is $0.65M–$8.7M; at full-area application, the range widens to $1.3M–$17.4M.[^8] A **priced** MWAA-wide stack — acoustic ceiling in the mandatory zones, FIDS redundancy across approximately 200 combined gates (MWAA counts vary by counting rule), and a mobile-notification platform — sizes to roughly $6M–$24M one-time. PA head-end reprogramming and commissioning-agent scope are components the priced exhibits do not cover; the conservative envelope including both is on the order of $40M.[^8] On the IAD-only basis, annualized at a 30-year AMT revenue-bond debt-service factor of about 7.5%, the priced range translates to approximately $0.02–$0.09 per IAD enplanement, or 0.10 to 0.43 percent of IAD's projected 2030 signatory CPE.[^8] The commercial-construction late-specification premium is 2× to 4×, not the 1:10:100 heuristic sometimes invoked — use the range you can defend.

## The Contrarian objection, answered

The strongest version of the case against writing anything new is this: NFPA 72, ADA/ACAA, ACRP 175, and a competent commissioning specification already require what a MWAA standard would require; the Design Manual may already carry acoustic language; the Board has not asked for this; and standards ossify while personal-audio devices, spatial notification, and app-based passenger communication mature inside the standard's first decade.

Each is true in its own sentence. None is true across the seam. NFPA 72 governs the fire-alarm envelope but not the routine paging matrix and not tenant audio; ACRP 175 recommends thresholds but binds no consultant scope; the ADA sets a redundancy floor but not a zoning grid; a commissioning specification tests one project. What the standard adds is *integration and enforcement* across the seam — commissioning acceptance in occupied conditions, re-verification after every tenant fit-out that penetrates the acoustic envelope, and a tenant-systems chapter that reaches airline podium audio and concession televisions. No single existing instrument delivers that. Whether the 2020 manual already contains acoustic criteria will shift the specification work from *write* to *bind, commission, re-verify, and reach tenants*, but the recommendation itself is robust to either finding. As for Board demand: the July 2026 announcement is the Board's question — what the Authority is going to procure with $20 billion. Specifying how that program gets scoped is the answer. And as for ossification: the standard is a Design Manual amendment on an annual update cycle, not a bond covenant. It is reversible in exactly the way a policy memo is not.

## The tiered decision

MWAA should authorize its Office of Engineering, in this Board officer term, to draft a Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard as an amendment to the Design Manual with companion Specification and Estimating & Scheduling sections. The first deliverable of the authorization is a diligence read of the 2020 manual to establish what already binds. Three tiers, one instrument:

**Mandatory across both airports.** Intelligibility floor at NFPA 72 / ACRP 175 thresholds, verified per IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions at TCO and after any envelope-penetrating tenant fit-out. Redundant channel obligation — audible, visual/digital, staffed — for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message. Independent generator and UPS backing for the ECS head-end and PA backbone. Federal accessibility compliance (49 CFR 27.71, 14 CFR 382.53, ADAAG 4.28.3) written as design criteria, not aspiration — sized to the direction of the DOT ACAA/PRM rulemaking, which is moving toward more real-time accessible information.[^13]

**Recommended, terminal-context permitting.** Zoned PA with pre-authorized override, tenant audio governance including gate podium and concessions, hearing loops at gates and information counters (the Port Authority of NY & NJ's Design-Manual precedent),[^23] sensory rooms at each hub (Pittsburgh, Atlanta, and Indianapolis are the peers; IAD is behind),[^24] and centralized non-routine announcement workflow on the SFO mechanism.

**Context-dependent, airport-specific.** DCA carries a higher operational announcement floor because slot loss is unrecoverable and post-1/29 safety messaging load is elevated. IAD carries a higher accessibility and language-support floor because 10.53 million of its 29.01 million 2025 passengers were international, a share above 36 percent.[^25] The standard should say so on the page, and specify variance procedures rather than pretend a single prescriptive number governs both.

Concourse E is the first testbed. If the standard is not written into the C/D replacement RFP scope, it will be argued back in as a change order, in front of an airline that will not be surprised to see it, at a cost multiple this article will not need to repeat. That is a decision, not a study. The Board's window to make it is now.

